# Vanilla JavaScript Frontend Project Rules
# Based on actual project structure and patterns

## Project Structure & Organization

### File Organization
- **Components**: Place reusable UI components in `src/components/` with index.js files
- **Pages**: Place page-level components in `src/pages/` with their own folders (e.g., `Login/login.js`, `Login/login.css`, `Login/login.html`)
- **Services**: Place business logic and API calls in `src/services/` organized by feature
- **API**: Place API client configurations and endpoints in `src/api/` organized by feature
- **Store**: Place state management in `src/store/` with separate store files per feature
- **Routes**: Place routing configuration in `src/routes/`
- **Utils**: Place reusable utilities in `src/utils/` with descriptive names (e.g., `storage.util.js`, `validators.util.js`)
- **Config**: Place configuration files in `src/config/` (e.g., `api.config.js`, `env.js`)
- **Styles**: Place global styles in `src/styles/` (e.g., `main.css`, `variables.css`, `reset.css`)
- **Tests**: Place tests in `src/tests/` organized by type (unit, e2e)

### Naming Conventions
- **Components**: Use PascalCase for component/class names (e.g., `Login`, `Navbar`, `Button`)
- **Files**: Use kebab-case for file names (e.g., `login.js`, `auth.service.js`, `storage.util.js`)
- **Folders**: Use kebab-case for folder names (e.g., `main-container`, `svg-components`)
- **Constants**: Use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for constant values (e.g., `API_STATUS.PENDING`, `USER_ROLES.ADMIN`)
- **Enums/Constants Objects**: Use PascalCase for constant object names (e.g., `APIStatus`, `UserRoles`, `Locations`)
- **Types/Interfaces**: Use PascalCase for type names (e.g., `UserData`, `AuthState`)
- **Functions**: Use camelCase for function names (e.g., `handleSubmit`, `validateEmail`, `getUserData`)
- **Variables**: Use camelCase for variable names (e.g., `userData`, `isLoading`, `errorMessage`)
- **CSS Classes**: Use underscores between words (e.g., `login_container`, `custom_button_primary`)

## Code Style & Formatting

### Indentation & Spacing
- Use tabs for indentation (not spaces)
- Set printWidth to 120 characters
- Use double quotes for strings
- Always include semicolons
- Use trailing commas in objects and arrays

### Import Organization
- Group imports in this order:
  1. External library imports (axios, etc.)
  2. Internal imports (relative paths)
  3. Style imports (CSS files)
- Use named imports when available
- Use default imports for modules that export a single default

### Module Structure
- Use ES6 modules (import/export)
- Place imports at the top
- Define constants before functions
- Place event handlers before render logic
- Export functions/components as named exports when possible
- Use default exports for main module exports

## JavaScript Standards

### Type Safety & Validation
- Define explicit JSDoc types for all function parameters and return values
- Use JSDoc comments for type documentation: `@param {Type} name - description`, `@returns {Type} description`
- Use interfaces/type objects for data structures (documented with JSDoc)
- Use enums/constant objects for fixed value sets
- Avoid using `any` or `unknown` without proper type guards
- **Null vs Undefined**: Always use `null` for optional/nullable values instead of `undefined` (e.g., `userData: UserData | null` not `userData: UserData | undefined`)

### Enum/Constant Creation Guidelines
- **Create Constants for Fixed Value Sets**: When you have a limited set of predefined values that won't change frequently
- **Location Constants**: For geographical locations (e.g., `Location.CHENNAI`, `Location.TRIVANDRUM`)
- **Status Constants**: For entity states (e.g., `Status.ACTIVE`, `Status.INACTIVE`)
- **API Status Constants**: For request states (e.g., `APIStatus.PENDING`, `APIStatus.FULFILLED`, `APIStatus.REJECTED`)
- **User Role Constants**: For user permission levels (e.g., `UserRoles.ADMIN`, `UserRoles.USER`)
- **Form Field Constants**: For form field identifiers and types (e.g., `FormFieldIds.NAME`, `FormFieldTypes.INPUT`)
- **Constant Naming**: Use PascalCase for constant object names, UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for constant values
- **Constant Location**: Place all constants in `src/utils/constants.js` for centralized access
- **Constant Usage**: Always use constant values instead of string literals in code
- **Constant Import**: Import constants from utils: `import { APIStatus, UserRoles } from "../utils/constants"`

### Data Structure Organization
- **Component Data Types**: Document component-specific data structures with JSDoc in the component file
- **Service Types**: Document service data structures with JSDoc in the service file
- **Shared Types**: Place shared data structures in `src/utils/constants.js` or create a `src/utils/types.js` file
- **Type Documentation**: Use JSDoc `@typedef` for complex types: `@typedef {Object} UserData`

## State Management

### Store Structure
- Organize stores by feature in `src/store/` (e.g., `auth.store.js`, `user.store.js`)
- Use a centralized store pattern with `src/store/store.js` as the main store
- Use proper state initialization
- Handle async actions with pending/fulfilled/rejected states
- Clear state appropriately after operations
- Use proper state getters and setters
- Document state structure with JSDoc

### Local State
- Use module-level variables for component-local state
- Use event listeners for side effects and user interactions
- Handle loading states properly
- Validate input before API calls
- Use proper state update patterns

## Component Patterns

### Page Components
- Each page should have its own folder (e.g., `Login/login.js`, `Login/login.css`, `Login/login.html`)
- Use class-based or functional module patterns
- Implement proper initialization methods
- Handle DOM manipulation efficiently
- Clean up event listeners on component destruction
- Use proper error handling

### Reusable Components
- Place reusable components in `src/components/` with their own folders
- Each component should have its own folder with `index.js`
- Components should be self-contained and reusable
- Use proper props/parameters for configuration
- Implement proper initialization and cleanup

### Form Components
- Implement proper validation with error states
- Use controlled inputs with state
- Handle keyboard events (Enter key for forms)
- Show validation errors below inputs
- **Form Field Constants**: Always use constants for form field IDs and types instead of string literals
- **Form Field Constant Pattern**: Create `FormFieldIds` and `FormFieldTypes` constants for each form
- **Form State Access**: Use constant values for form state access (e.g., `form[FormFieldIds.EMAIL]`)
- **Form Validation**: Use constant values in validation functions for field access and error handling
- **Form Field Configuration**: Use constants in form field definitions (e.g., `id: FormFieldIds.NAME`)

### Table Components
- Create separate column definition files for table headers instead of defining them inline
- Define proper table headers with sorting and resizing
- Handle pagination with proper state management
- Implement row click handlers when needed
- Use proper loading states
- Import column definitions: `import { tableHeaders } from "./column"`

## Styling Standards

### CSS Organization
- Use component-specific CSS files (e.g., `login.css`, `header.css`)
- Import CSS files in the component/module files
- Use BEM-like naming conventions for CSS classes
- Maintain consistent spacing and layout patterns
- **No Inline Styles**: Never use inline CSS with `style=""` attributes - always use CSS classes or CSS variables
- Use CSS variables from `src/styles/variables.css` for theming

### CSS Classes
- Use descriptive class names with underscores between words (e.g., `login_full_container`, `custom_table_container`)
- Follow existing naming patterns in the project
- **Class Naming**: Always use underscores (_) between words in CSS class names, not hyphens (-)
- **Color Variables**: Always use CSS variables from `src/styles/variables.css` instead of hardcoded colors
- **New Colors**: If a color is not present in `variables.css`, add it as a variable first, then use it
- **No Gap Property**: Use `margin` properties instead of `gap` for spacing
- **Color Consolidation**: Never create duplicate color variables with the same hex values - always reuse existing variables
- **Color Duplication Check**: Before adding new color variables, check if the same hex value already exists in `variables.css`
- **Color Variable Naming**: Use descriptive names that indicate the color's purpose (e.g., `--textPrimary`, `--iconColor`, `--statusActiveBg`)
- **Flex Property**: Always define complete flex property values instead of shorthand (e.g., use `flex: 1 1 0` instead of `flex: 1`)
- **Font Weight**: Use font-family names instead of numeric font-weight values (e.g., use `font-family: "bold"` instead of `font-weight: 600`)
- **CSS Spacing**: Do not add blank lines between CSS properties within the same selector - keep properties grouped together
- **CSS Zero Values**: Avoid unnecessary CSS properties with zero values (e.g., don't use `min-width: 0`, `margin: 0`, `padding: 0` unless specifically needed for layout)
- **CSS Margin/Padding Shorthand**: Never use margin/padding shorthand when most values are zero - specify only the specific property that has a value (e.g., use `margin-bottom: 5px` instead of `margin: 0 0 5px 0`)
- **Viewport Units**: Avoid using viewport units (vh, vw) for height and width - use percentage values instead

## Product-Specific Rules (Event Photo Management)

### Design Identity (Studio White SaaS)
- **Backgrounds**: Always use pure white (`#FFFFFF`) or soft off-white (`#F8FAFC`). Do not use dark themes.
- **Colors**: Use CSS variables from `src/styles/variables.css`. Define `--brandIndigo: #4F46E5;` and `--brandGreen: #10B981;` if not present.
- **Spacing**: Follow the "No Gap Property" rule; use `margin-bottom` or `margin-right` instead of `gap`.
- **Typography**: Use `font-family: "bold"` or `"medium"` instead of numeric weights like 600 or 500.
- **UI Aesthetics**: Create clean, minimal interfaces with ample white space and subtle shadows for depth.
- **Button Styles**: Use brand colors (indigo/green) for primary actions, with proper hover states and transitions.
- **Card Components**: Use subtle borders or shadows to create visual separation without heavy borders.
- **Form Inputs**: Use clean, modern input styles with proper focus states using brand colors.

### Feature-Specific Implementation

#### Google Drive Integration
- Place the Google Auth logic in `src/services/drive.service.js` or `src/services/google-drive/` folder structure.
- Use `APIStatus` constants for folder creation and upload states.
- Handle OAuth flow with proper error states and user feedback.
- Display authorization status clearly in the UI.

#### Event Management
- Use the Form Field Enum Pattern for event creation. Create `EventFormFieldIds` (NAME, DATE, FOLDER_ID) and `EventFormFieldTypes` in `src/utils/constants.js`.
- Display events using table components with proper column definitions in separate files.
- Use clean, organized layouts for event lists and creation forms.
- Show event status and metadata clearly with proper visual hierarchy.

#### Guest Portal (Public)
- Use `src/pages/Guest/` for guest portal pages (e.g., `QRScan/`, `SelfieCapture/`, `PhotoResults/`).
- Implement the camera/selfie capture using proper MediaDevices API with error handling.
- Match results must be displayed in a grid using CSS for layout (no viewport units; use percentages).
- Create intuitive, user-friendly interfaces for guests with clear instructions.
- Use responsive grid layouts for photo results with proper image loading states.
- Implement proper loading indicators during face matching process.

#### Image Upload & Display
- Show upload progress with visual indicators using brand colors.
- Display images in clean, organized grids with proper aspect ratios.
- Use lazy loading for image galleries to improve performance.
- Show proper error states when images fail to load.

### Data Standards
- **Biometrics**: Never store guest images in the backend. Only send to the processing service and store the resulting numeric vector.
- **Null Safety**: Always use `null` for optional event data or missing face matches; never `undefined`.
- **Privacy**: Ensure guest data is handled securely and images are not persisted unnecessarily.
- **Data Validation**: Validate all image uploads for type and size before processing.

## API & Data Handling

### Service Layer
- Organize services by feature in `src/services/`
- Use async/await for API calls
- Handle errors properly with try/catch
- Use proper error handling and user feedback
- Implement proper loading states
- Document API response structures with JSDoc

### API Client
- Use axios instance from `src/api/axiosInstance.js` for API calls
- Organize API endpoints by feature in `src/api/` (e.g., `auth.api.js`, `user.api.js`)
- Use proper error handling for API calls
- Implement request/response interceptors when needed
- Document API endpoints and their parameters

### Constants & Configuration
- Define API endpoints and formats in `src/utils/constants.js`
- Use regex patterns for validation (email, password, etc.) in `src/utils/validators.util.js`
- Define date formats consistently
- Use constants for status values
- Store configuration in `src/config/` files

## Error Handling

### Error Management
- Handle API errors gracefully
- Show user-friendly error messages
- Log errors appropriately (use console.error for debugging)
- Implement proper error boundaries for critical operations
- Use try/catch blocks for async operations

### Validation
- Implement client-side validation before API calls
- Use regex patterns for input validation (store in `src/utils/validators.util.js`)
- Show validation errors immediately
- Clear errors when user starts typing
- Validate all user inputs before submission

## Performance & Best Practices

### JavaScript Patterns
- Use proper event delegation when possible
- Avoid memory leaks by cleaning up event listeners
- Use debouncing/throttling for frequent events (scroll, resize, input)
- Cache DOM queries when possible
- Use proper async/await patterns
- Avoid blocking the main thread with heavy operations

### Code Quality
- Follow ESLint configurations
- Use modern ES6+ features
- Implement proper error boundaries
- Use proper loading states
- Write clean, readable, and maintainable code
- Add JSDoc comments for complex functions

## File Structure Examples

### Component File Structure
```javascript
import { APIStatus } from "../utils/constants";
import "./component.css";

/**
 * Component class for handling component functionality
 * @class Component
 */
class Component {
  constructor(container) {
    this.container = container;
    this.state = {
      isLoading: false,
      data: null,
    };
    this.init();
  }

  init() {
    this.render();
    this.attachEventListeners();
  }

  render() {
    // Render logic here
  }

  attachEventListeners() {
    // Event listener setup
  }

  cleanup() {
    // Cleanup event listeners
  }
}

export default Component;
```

### Form Component File Structure with Constants
```javascript
import { FormFieldIds, FormFieldTypes, emailRegex, phoneNumberRegex } from "../utils/constants";
import { validateEmail, validatePhone } from "../utils/validators.util";
import "./form.css";

/**
 * Form state structure
 * @typedef {Object} FormState
 * @property {string} [FormFieldIds.NAME] - User name
 * @property {string} [FormFieldIds.EMAIL] - User email
 * @property {string} [FormFieldIds.PHONE_NUMBER] - User phone number
 */

class FormComponent {
  constructor(container) {
    this.container = container;
    this.form = {
      [FormFieldIds.NAME]: "",
      [FormFieldIds.EMAIL]: "",
      [FormFieldIds.PHONE_NUMBER]: "",
    };
    this.errors = {};
    this.init();
  }

  init() {
    this.render();
    this.attachEventListeners();
  }

  /**
   * Validates the form
   * @returns {boolean} - True if form is valid
   */
  validate() {
    if (!this.form[FormFieldIds.EMAIL]?.trim()) {
      this.errors[FormFieldIds.EMAIL] = "Email is required";
      return false;
    }
    if (!emailRegex.test(this.form[FormFieldIds.EMAIL].trim())) {
      this.errors[FormFieldIds.EMAIL] = "Invalid email format";
      return false;
    }
    return true;
  }

  handleSubmit(event) {
    event.preventDefault();
    if (this.validate()) {
      // Submit form
    }
  }

  render() {
    // Render form
  }

  attachEventListeners() {
    // Event listeners
  }
}

export default FormComponent;
```

### Service File Structure
```javascript
import axiosInstance from "../api/axiosInstance";
import { APIStatus } from "../utils/constants";

/**
 * Service state structure
 * @typedef {Object} ServiceState
 * @property {string} status - API status
 * @property {Object|null} data - Service data
 * @property {string|null} error - Error message
 */

class Service {
  constructor() {
    this.state = {
      status: APIStatus.IDLE,
      data: null,
      error: null,
    };
  }

  /**
   * Fetches data from API
   * @returns {Promise<Object>} - API response
   */
  async fetchData() {
    try {
      this.state.status = APIStatus.PENDING;
      const response = await axiosInstance.get("/endpoint");
      this.state.data = response.data;
      this.state.status = APIStatus.FULFILLED;
      return response.data;
    } catch (error) {
      this.state.error = error.message;
      this.state.status = APIStatus.REJECTED;
      throw error;
    }
  }
}

export default new Service();
```

### Store File Structure
```javascript
import { APIStatus } from "../utils/constants";

/**
 * Auth state structure
 * @typedef {Object} AuthState
 * @property {boolean} isAuthenticated - Authentication status
 * @property {Object|null} user - User data
 * @property {string} status - API status
 */
class AuthStore {
  constructor() {
    this.state = {
      isAuthenticated: false,
      user: null,
      status: APIStatus.IDLE,
    };
    this.listeners = [];
  }

  /**
   * Subscribes to state changes
   * @param {Function} callback - Callback function
   * @returns {Function} - Unsubscribe function
   */
  subscribe(callback) {
    this.listeners.push(callback);
    return () => {
      this.listeners = this.listeners.filter((listener) => listener !== callback);
    };
  }

  /**
   * Notifies all listeners of state changes
   */
  notify() {
    this.listeners.forEach((callback) => callback(this.state));
  }

  /**
   * Updates the state
   * @param {Partial<AuthState>} updates - State updates
   */
  setState(updates) {
    this.state = { ...this.state, ...updates };
    this.notify();
  }
}

export default new AuthStore();
```

### API File Structure
```javascript
import axiosInstance from "./axiosInstance";

/**
 * User API endpoints
 */
const userAPI = {
  /**
   * Fetches user data
   * @param {string} userId - User ID
   * @returns {Promise<Object>} - User data
   */
  async getUser(userId) {
    const response = await axiosInstance.get(`/users/${userId}`);
    return response.data;
  },

  /**
   * Updates user data
   * @param {string} userId - User ID
   * @param {Object} userData - User data to update
   * @returns {Promise<Object>} - Updated user data
   */
  async updateUser(userId, userData) {
    const response = await axiosInstance.put(`/users/${userId}`, userData);
    return response.data;
  },
};

export default userAPI;
```

## Enforcement Rules

### Must Follow
- Use tabs for indentation
- Use double quotes for strings
- Always include semicolons
- Use proper JSDoc type documentation
- Follow existing naming conventions
- Use component-specific CSS files
- Implement proper error handling
- Use ES6 modules (import/export)
- Place component types in JSDoc comments
- Use CSS classes instead of inline styles
- Create constants for fixed value sets (locations, statuses, roles, etc.)
- Use constant values instead of string literals in code
- Place all constants in `src/utils/constants.js` for centralized access
- **Form Field Constants**: Always create and use constants for form field IDs and types
- **Form Field Constant Pattern**: Create `FormFieldIds` and `FormFieldTypes` constants for each form
- **Form State Access**: Use constant values for form state access (e.g., `form[FormFieldIds.EMAIL]`)
- **Form Validation**: Use constant values in validation functions for field access and error handling
- **Font Weight**: Use font-family names instead of numeric font-weight values
- **CSS Spacing**: Keep CSS properties grouped together without blank lines within the same selector
- **CSS Zero Values**: Only use zero values when specifically needed for layout behavior
- **CSS Margin/Padding Shorthand**: Never use margin/padding shorthand when most values are zero
- **Viewport Units**: Use percentage values instead of viewport units (vh, vw) for height and width
- **Null Standard**: Always use `null` for optional/nullable values, never use `undefined` in type definitions
- **Design Identity**: Always use pure white (`#FFFFFF`) or soft off-white (`#F8FAFC`) backgrounds - no dark themes
- **Brand Colors**: Use `--brandIndigo: #4F46E5` and `--brandGreen: #10B981` from CSS variables for primary actions
- **UI Spacing**: Use `margin-bottom` or `margin-right` instead of `gap` property for spacing
- **Typography**: Use `font-family: "bold"` or `"medium"` instead of numeric font-weight values
- **Event Form Constants**: Create `EventFormFieldIds` and `EventFormFieldTypes` constants for event forms
- **Guest Portal Layout**: Use percentage-based responsive grids for photo results, not viewport units
- **Biometric Privacy**: Never store guest images in backend - only send to processing service and store numeric vectors
- **Image Validation**: Always validate image type and size before upload

### Must Not Do
- Use spaces instead of tabs
- Use single quotes for strings
- Omit semicolons
- Use `any` or `unknown` without proper type guards
- Create components outside proper directories
- Mix inline styles with existing CSS setup
- Ignore JavaScript errors
- Use global variables without proper namespacing
- Define types without JSDoc documentation
- Use inline CSS with `style=""` attributes
- Use string literals instead of constants for fixed value sets
- Create constants in component files or separate constant files
- Use hardcoded values when constants are available
- **Form Field Anti-patterns**: Use string literals for form field IDs (e.g., `"name"`, `"email"`) instead of constants
- **Form Field Anti-patterns**: Use string literals for form field types (e.g., `"input"`, `"select"`) instead of constants
- **Form Field Anti-patterns**: Access form state with string literals (e.g., `form["email"]`) instead of constant values
- **Form Field Anti-patterns**: Define form field configurations with string literals instead of constant values
- **Font Weight Anti-patterns**: Use numeric font-weight values (500, 600, 700, etc.) instead of font-family names
- **CSS Spacing Anti-patterns**: Add blank lines between CSS properties within the same selector
- **CSS Zero Values Anti-patterns**: Use unnecessary zero values when not needed for layout
- **CSS Margin/Padding Shorthand Anti-patterns**: Use margin/padding shorthand with mostly zero values
- **Viewport Units Anti-patterns**: Use viewport units (vh, vw) instead of percentage values
- **Color Anti-patterns**: Create duplicate color variables with the same hex values
- **Color Anti-patterns**: Use hardcoded hex colors in CSS files when CSS variables exist
- **Color Anti-patterns**: Use generic color names instead of descriptive names
- **Null Anti-patterns**: Use `undefined` in type definitions for optional/nullable values instead of `null`
- **Design Anti-patterns**: Use dark themes or backgrounds other than white/off-white
- **Design Anti-patterns**: Use hardcoded brand colors instead of CSS variables
- **Design Anti-patterns**: Use `gap` property instead of margin properties for spacing
- **Design Anti-patterns**: Use numeric font-weight values (500, 600, 700) instead of font-family names
- **Event Form Anti-patterns**: Use string literals for event form field IDs instead of constants
- **Guest Portal Anti-patterns**: Use viewport units (vh, vw) for photo grid layouts instead of percentages
- **Biometric Anti-patterns**: Store guest images in backend or client storage
- **Image Upload Anti-patterns**: Upload images without validating type and size first

